WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.475
Subagents: isolation, not savings.

2
00:00:02.755 --> 00:00:07.107
A subagent is a separate agent loop with its own fresh context window.

3
00:00:07.387 --> 00:00:19.141
The lead agent hands it a task (“find every open risk in the vendor documents”), the subagent spends as many tokens as it needs searching and reading, and it returns a condensed result.

4
00:00:19.421 --> 00:00:32.435
Anthropic’s context engineering post describes subagents that may use tens of thousands of tokens exploring and return summaries of about 1,000 to 2,000 tokens.

5
00:00:32.715 --> 00:00:36.704
The lead’s context grows by the summary, not by the exploration.

6
00:00:36.984 --> 00:00:41.699
Two cautions from Anthropic’s multi-agent research post keep this honest.

7
00:00:41.979 --> 00:00:58.661
First, cost: they report agents using about four times the tokens of a chat interaction and multi-agent systems about fifteen times, and they say multi-agent setups pay off mainly on valuable tasks with lots of parallelizable, separable work.

8
00:00:58.941 --> 00:01:09.864
Second, fit: tasks where every agent needs shared context, or with many dependencies between subtasks, are poor fits, and they name most coding tasks as examples.

9
00:01:10.144 --> 00:01:20.597
The same post suggests that subagents write large outputs to a file system and pass back lightweight references, so the lead does not have to copy them through its context.

10
00:01:20.877 --> 00:01:32.291
For model selection, subagents are where cheaper models and lower effort often fit: reading-heavy, well-scoped subtasks can run on a smaller model while the lead stays on the strongest one.

11
00:01:32.571 --> 00:01:39.760
Measure it on your tasks before assuming it helps, and remember that a subagent’s summary is model output the lead will trust.

12
00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:47.635
If the summary is the evidence for a decision, ask the subagent to include the source ids it relied on, and spot-check them.
